WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:15.100
This is a ciliate, Loxodes magnus.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.400
It is about to die.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:23.640
Of course, depending on your time scale...we’re
all about to die.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:30.500
To the grand canyon, or the sun...things that
have existed for millions or billions of years,

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:37.791
we are each weird little bubbles of peculiar
chemistry that form and then pop...form, and

00:00:37.791 --> 00:00:40.710
then pop.

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:45.739
But this ciliate, and with our new microscope
you can really see those cilia beating, is

00:00:45.739 --> 00:00:49.039
about to pop right before your eyes.

00:00:49.039 --> 00:00:51.410
It looks fine right now.

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:56.079
You can even see, inside it, it’s last meal,
a Trachelomonas.

00:00:56.079 --> 00:00:59.950
So we don’t think it’s starving to death.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.449
It seems to be trucking along just fine.

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:09.140
Loxodes Magnus are microaerophilic organisms,
preferring a low concentration of dissolved

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.850
oxygen in their environment, but not too low.

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:19.000
So maybe the concentration on the slide was
too high, though we’ve witnessed many others

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.440
who have been just fine in our preparations.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:29.460
So no, we can’t tell you why this ciliate
is about to die...but we can tell you that

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:37.830
right here...that’s where James, our master
of microscopes, first saw something strange.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:44.690
The moment the ciliate shifted direction,
a little trail of cell membrane and cytoplasm.

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:46.690
No reason.

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:49.550
Nothing grabbed it, it didn’t snag on anything.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:56.900
But a little bit of what was once a part of
the organism was suddenly, no longer a part

00:01:56.900 --> 00:01:58.030
of it.

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:04.670
That cytoplasm is full of complicated molecules
that are what chemists would call, far from

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:09.709
equilibrium.

00:02:09.709 --> 00:02:15.640
Equilibrium is the situation in which chemicals
no longer have a tendency to react over time.

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:23.110
In general, a thing that you can say for sure
is that all the stuff outside of living cells

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.690
is either at chemical equilibrium, or it is
headed there.

00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:35.730
Whereas stuff inside cells is not at equilibrium,
and it’s not headed there either.

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:44.250
How are all of these chemicals that, if left
alone, would rapidly reach equilibrium managing

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.560
to not do that?

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.520
Life.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:52.130
That is what life is.

00:02:52.130 --> 00:03:05.370
A bunch of chemicals that take in energy in
order to keep each other from reaching equilibrium.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:12.080
Quick break from our friend...the way we define
life in biology classes is...wrong.

00:03:12.080 --> 00:03:17.819
It’s not even really a definition, it’s
a set of qualifying factors.

00:03:17.819 --> 00:03:19.819
Life has to take in energy.

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:27.180
Life has to reproduce, it must respond to
its environment, it must consist of cells.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:33.730
This is not a definition, it’s an attempt
to draw a line, to create a boundary.

00:03:33.730 --> 00:03:40.350
And that makes sense for things that are actually
amorphous and complicated, like social constructs.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:47.200
But life is not a construct of our opinions,
but of reality.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:56.550
Life is a chemical system that uses energy
to keep itself from reaching chemical equilibrium.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:58.400
Why do they do it?

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:02.730
Oh, well maybe let’s not go /that/ deep,
at least not today.

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:09.170
Suffice it to say, a system that did this
developed on this planet and now, billions

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:15.040
of years later, it is still doing it.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:20.410
We have many things in common with this ciliate,
and not to belabor the point, but one of those

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.210
things is that we will die.

00:04:23.210 --> 00:04:30.530
You’ve probablt noticed by now that this
video isn’t about what life is, it’s about

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.030
what death is.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:36.250
It’s just that, first, we had to define
life.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:42.160
Life is chemicals working together to take
in energy to keep themselves far from equilibrium.

00:04:42.160 --> 00:04:46.620
Death is not the return to chemical equilibrium.

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:50.040
The process of decay can last decades.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:56.500
Likewise, many parts of my body will return
to equilibrium over the course of my life,

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:00.990
I’m shedding skin cells right now and so
are you.

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:08.430
The atoms and molecules of my body are replaced
with new ones over and over and over again.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.360
But I will only die once.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:18.770
Likewise, our ciliate has been shedding cytoplasm
and cell membrane for minutes now, and that

00:05:18.770 --> 00:05:22.880
shed cytoplasm is dead, no doubt.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.370
But the organism lives.

00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.120
Its chemistry continues.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:30.600
For now.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:39.750
Death is the moment when the system that maintains
the far from equilibrium state ceases existence.

00:05:39.750 --> 00:05:42.640
And we can imagine that at many scales.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:48.080
That can happen to individual bits of an organism,
as it is happening to the chemicals spilling

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:50.480
out of our Loxodes right now.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:54.430
It can also happen to an individual cell in
an organism.

00:05:54.430 --> 00:05:55.440
And that happens all the time.

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:59.490
It is happening right now inside you.

00:05:59.490 --> 00:06:02.050
It can also happen to an organism.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:07.940
That’s what we usually think of as death,
with our focus, so often, on the individual.

00:06:07.940 --> 00:06:12.990
But we can keep moving up the scale and find
yet other kinds of death.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:18.600
When a common genetic system that was useful
for keeping many similar but individual organisms

00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:22.182
alive ceases to exist, that is an extinction.

00:06:22.182 --> 00:06:25.449
A kind of death.

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:31.090
And when the system that has kept all life
on earth far from equilibrium for billions

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:38.060
of years, that system that we all share of
nucleic and amino acids, when that ceases

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:42.210
to exist, that will be something else.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:48.150
A terrible kind of death that we do not even
have a name for.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:50.320
But it will be a death.

00:06:50.320 --> 00:06:57.210
The largest death, I suppose, until heat death,
when everything in the universe has found

00:06:57.210 --> 00:07:10.740
equilibrium.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:13.440
Our ciliate is about out of time now.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:19.410
I don’t know when we can call it, when we
can pronounce the time of death, but this

00:07:19.410 --> 00:07:22.240
seems as good a time as any.

00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:24.530
Here, we have death.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:39.740
The system that was using energy to keep itself
from reaching equilibrium has ceased to exist.

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:45.490
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds

00:07:45.490 --> 00:07:46.490
us.

00:07:46.490 --> 00:07:52.430
And thank you as always, and of course to
all of the people on the screen right now.

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:57.680
These are our Patreon patrons without whom
